>> title: Tamagoyaki
>> category: Eggs
>> tags: japanese, egg, dashi, side, bento, quick
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 卵焼き, tamagoyaki, tamagoyaki, たまごやき, 玉子焼き, だし巻き卵, dashimaki tamago, rolled omelette, Japanese rolled egg
>> pairs-with: dashi, gohan, shogayaki
>> servings: 4
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: unforgiving — a pan hot enough to brown the first layer browns every layer after it, and the stripes show through the finished roll
>> step.1: This is the だし巻き, the dashi version — savoury, loose, eaten warm or cold. The sweet Kantō one, with a tablespoon of sugar an egg, is a different recipe cooked at a different heat, and a file that does not say which one it is cannot be followed.
>> step.2: whisk and strain
>> step.3: pour the first third, set 40 sec, roll
>> step.4: roll two more thirds over the top
>> step.5: shape in the mat, rest 5 min

This is the だし巻き, the dashi version — savoury, loose enough to wobble, eaten warm or cold out of a lunchbox. The sweet Kantō one, with about a tablespoon of sugar an egg, is a different recipe cooked at a different heat. A file that does not say which one it is cannot be followed, so: this one.

Whisk @eggs{4} with @dashi{4%Tbs}(60 mL), @usukuchi soy sauce{1%tsp}(5 mL), @mirin{1%tsp}(5 mL), @sugar{1%tsp} and @fine sea salt{1%pinch} in a #bowl{}, then pour it through a sieve. The sieve takes out the chalazae, and it is the whole reason a good tamagoyaki has no white streaks in the cut face.

Wipe a #tamagoyaki pan{} with @neutral oil{2%tsp}(10 mL) on a folded paper towel over medium-low heat, pour a third of @&(~1)egg{} in, and ~set{40%sec}, until the top is barely wet, then roll it away from you into a log at the far end.

Pour half of what is left down the pan and lift @&(~1)roll{} so it runs underneath, ~set{40%sec}, roll it back over the top, and do the same with the last of the egg.

Turn @&(~1)tamagoyaki{} onto a #bamboo mat{}, roll it up and squeeze it gently square, and let it ~rest{5%min} before cutting it into eight.
